all right let's check out this 3m cup  mask you know is it legit or is it not  let's find out this is an n95 mask the  same type the biden administration has  begun distributing for free oh the  concentration is coming way down here so  that's a that's a really good sign  n95 is a type of respirator mask kn-95  is another it's very comfortable i have  a good fit here properly fitted masks  like these can provide better protection  from the particles that carry coved if  everybody was wearing a respirator  during a surge you would stun  transmission because you have two-way  protection approaching 95 percent that's  95 percent of particles blocked from  being inhaled and exhaled too thus the  95 of the names but that's only when  these respirator masks perform properly  i want to make sure i get a good seal on  it problem is it's tough to know if your  mask actually has 95 filtration  efficiency a search on amazon where  masks have been a top search term  through the pandemic turns up thousands  of results and underperforming masks can  creep in and notice that concentration  coming down but not as low as i'd like  to see it  so what can you do to find a good mask  and how easy is it to accidentally get a  bad one  to find out we bought some popular maths  on amazon and put them to the test with  experts that's that difference between  there and there is the sign of a good  mask or a fake mask this is aaron  collins also known as the mask nerd so  these are my collection of masks i feel  like i have every mass that you might be  able to get from south korea or in the  united states right now collins is a  mechanical engineer with a background in  aerosol science he says spotting poor  performing masks is tough because they  can look identical to the good ones that  is the real crux of the problem is you  can have a good mask or have a bad mask  and you won't know his solution testing  masks live on youtube from his home lab  which is his bathroom if you just are  given a mask and you don't know anything  about mask you're gonna think wow i got  99 95 protection  so we asked collins to do the same for  us with the three n95s and the five  kn95s we ordered from amazon all of the  kn95s were listed as top 10 best sellers  and one of the n95s was labeled as an  amazon choice at the time of purchase  the n95s we ordered all received  certification from the national  institute for occupational safety and  health or niosh we boxed up samples of  the eight mast and sent them off to  collins for testing the test that i do  is what's uh called a total inward  leakage test what you need for this type  of measurement is two things you need an  aerosol source and a particle counter  without getting too technical collins  uses these two machines to test the  filtration efficiency of the masks to  show how they perform as he wears them  the results appear on his laptop as he  tests  basically the more this line falls the  better the mask is at filtering outside  aerosols  one thing you'll notice right away is  that that concentration has now dropped  way down this indicates that it's very  likely a legitimate mask  for his test collins is looking for the  highest filtration percentage possible  he says n95s and kn95s typically reach  about 97 or higher  of the eight masks we sent him six  performed well the one that was labeled  amazon choice this 3m and 95 reached  99.7  all right well 3m ones that i got  definitely real but two of the masks we  sent did not reach the filtration  efficiency collins typically sees oh  not looking good this one a kn 95  labeled wanway marked as an amazon best  seller yeah 88  that there my friends  is most likely a very underperforming kf  okay at 95 sorry  and this one i'm doubtful a niosh  approved n95 mask from aeropro which  came in at 92.5 percent in collins test  i'm not convinced that this is real  either that's pretty low performing so  after getting these results the journal  sent both underperforming masks as well  as the 3m and 95 that tested well to 4c  air a lab that performs the tests  required for mass to receive niosh  approval  five samples of each mask were tested  all five 3m masks tested with a  filtration efficiency above 99 percent  which 4c air set is in line with most  n95s it tests but only one of the  aeropro masks met the 95 threshold the  rest fell short with the mask averaging  just over 93 filtration efficiency the  lab said this indicates this batch would  likely not earn niosh approval as none  can fall below 95 percent niosh said it  can't comment on the results as the  testing was not done in its lab  all of the masts labeled want wayne  vastly underperformed averaging 81.9  percent with the lowest filtration  efficiency at 77.5  based on these tests 4c air said this  mask would fall short of the can 95  standard both aeropro and the company  that sells the wan wayne labeled masks  did not respond to multiple requests for  comment in a statement amazon said we  take credible allegations of  non-compliance seriously conduct  internal investigations and hold bad  actors accountable for violating amazon  rules and policies after the journal  reached out to amazon for comment the  aeropro mask listing had been removed  and after the journal shared the test  results with amazon the wan wayne mask  was still available for purchase the  white version was listed as an amazon  choice  so are these masks fake that's tough to  say there's still masks that filter  particles but not at the 95 filtration  efficiency required by the n95 and kn95  standards  and that leaves wearers less protected  than they think  so what can you do if you have a bad  mask  well you can try opening one up let's go  ahead and take a look in here so the  filtration layer is this layer here this  is the melt blown electrostatically  charged polymer layer it's polypropylene  while the exact number of layers in a  respirator mask may differ the one to  look for is this filter layer aka the  melt blown electrostatically charged  polymer layer and you can see there's  you can sort of pull that apart and see  the sort of intricate design that goes  into a filter it's that filter that  traps the smallest aerosol particles the  kind that can carry the virus  but seeing the filter isn't enough to  know for sure that it's a legit mask  either the aeropro and the wayne  labelled masks for example both had  filtration collins says there are other  clues you can look for to spot a  possibly poor mask starting with the  labeling on the outside they got most of  the canon 95 standard requirements  although they did not get  the manufacturer name on there make sure  the mask is trying to at least meet some  of the standard requirement there are  labeling requirements as part of the can  95 standard kn95s should say kn95 they  should also say gb 2626-2006  or 2019. lastly they should say the  manufacturer's name if you just see the  kn95 stamp on there you already know  that that mask doesn't meet the  requirements of the kn-95 standard so  what else does it not meet none of the  can 95 masks we tested even the high  performing ones had all of this  information on the mask and that colin  said adds confusion for consumers  the centers for disease control and  prevention keeps a list of known  counterfeit km95s or those  misrepresenting niosh approval the n95  labeling requirements are much more  stringent for these masks they also need  to say n95 they need to have the niosh  logo or text on there as well as the  approval number starting with t and c  they also must have the manufacturer's  name you can use the information printed  on the mask to verify its niosh approval  against the list on the cdc's website  still that isn't foolproof either the  aeropro n95 met these labeling  requirements and appeared on the niosh  approval list for example  here collins has another tip check the  supplier if you're buying from a big box  retailer or you're buying from the  manufacturer's website or through their  amazon web server so if you go to like  3m's direct site the probability of  getting a fake i think is really much  lower because of that niosh requirement  and the regulation that happens around  it in the case of the aeropro mask the  seller was accunet a medical supply  company that also sells third-party  masks in a statement accumed said that  as a distributor of niosh n95  respirators we place our trust in the  cdc niosh and the fda to validate the  manufacturers listed on their websites  as approved accumed said it also plans  to start independently testing the mass  itself it no longer sells the aeropro  mask the journal purchased niosh said it  will add the aeropro mask to the list of  the products it plans to audit this year  i think it gets much harder to be  trusting of the brands without some  piece of third-party evidence that the  mass that they're supplying is good and  i don't see that on amazon listings i  don't see a third party that has tested  the mask and said this is the results  amazon said it has a rigorous seller  vetting and product review process for  n95 and kn95s that involves comparing  masks against the cdc's counterfeit mask  list and the niosh approval list as well  as obtaining test results from an  accredited lab in china for kn95 masks  when it comes to third-party sellers  collin suggests project n95 a non-profit  that only sells masks it is vetted or  there's one other option and that's not  to shop at all the biden administration  has started distributing 400 million  n95s to pharmacies and community health  centers across the country the mass are  free and limited to three per customer  i'll be honest i wish this happened in  january 2021 but it's better late than  never we we will deal with more variance  we'll deal with more epidemic surges in  the future and  the bar has now been raised the bar has  been raised to n95 mask this is  critically important  [Music]

